# Environment variables — what goes where.
#
# The tracked `.env` holds PUBLIC values only: the Supabase project id, the project URL, and
# the publishable key. All three are designed to be public — they ship in the browser bundle
# and are protected by row-level security, not by secrecy. That file stays in git.
#
# Any SECRET — the Supabase service-role key, provider API keys (Anthropic, Stripe, GitHub,
# Linear, Lovable), webhook signing secrets — goes in `.env.local`, which is git-ignored and
# is never committed. If a value would do damage in a stranger's hands, it belongs there.
#
# WHY `.env` STAYS TRACKED, against Supabase's own advice (founder decision, 2026-07-29).
# Supabase documents that `config.toml` can pull secrets via `env(NAME)` from the root `.env`,
# and therefore says do not commit it. We keep it tracked anyway, for one reason: Lovable owns
# the frontend and its build needs those VITE_ values present in the repo. The tradeoff is
# accepted knowingly, with two mitigations:
#   1. The test harness no longer inherits the parent environment — it passes an allowlisted
#      env to its children, so a secret sitting in `.env`/`.env.local` cannot reach a test and
#      from there reach the hosted project. (See tests/at/harness/runner.ts.)
#   2. The rule above is the discipline that keeps it safe: NOTHING secret goes in `.env`.
#      If a Supabase feature ever needs `env()` interpolation for a real secret, that is the
#      moment to revisit this decision — not before.
#
# This file lists the variable names only, with empty values, as the shape to copy.

# AT_JUDGE_API_KEY is PARENT-SIDE ONLY, and that is a design decision rather than a convention.
# It is the credential the acceptance harness's semantic oracle (H4) uses to call the judge model,
# and it is read by exactly two things, both of which run beside you rather than inside a test:
# `tests/at/harness/record-oracles.ts` and the smoke it writes. The test runner's environment
# allowlist does NOT carry it, so it cannot reach a spawned test process — which means it belongs
# in your shell or in `.env.local`, never anywhere a child could inherit it. How a judge credential
# should reach a child at integration tier is deliberately undecided; that call belongs to the
# slice that makes the integration tier real, taken with its first consuming run.
# The judge is parked under loop/parked/v1/; nothing live reads this variable.
AT_JUDGE_API_KEY=

# The Google sign-in credential. `supabase/config.toml` reads both names through `env(...)`, so the
# local stack will not start with a Google provider block that references nothing.
#
# THE SECRET GOES IN THE GIT-IGNORED `.env.local`, NEVER IN THE TRACKED `.env` — the rule at the top
# of this file, applied to the first value that has ever tested it. The client id is not itself a
# secret, but keeping the pair together is what stops the secret being pasted beside it later.
#
# A PLACEHOLDER IS ENOUGH TO START THE STACK, AND IS NOT A CREDENTIAL. With any non-empty value the
# provider block is well-formed and `/auth/v1/settings` reports Google enabled — that and nothing
# more. It proves nothing about the handshake, because the local Auth server builds its authorize
# URL from whatever is configured without ever contacting Google. A real client id ends in
# `.apps.googleusercontent.com`; creating one is a manual step, and until it is done the handshake
# check in `loop/items/AI4DEV-57/proof-local.ts` reports itself SKIPPED rather than passed.
SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# The GitHub sign-in credential, on exactly the same terms as Google above: both names are read by
# `supabase/config.toml` through `env(...)`, the secret belongs in the git-ignored `.env.local`, and
# a placeholder starts the stack while proving nothing about the handshake.
#
# THE CALLBACK URL IS THE PART THAT IS EASY TO GET WRONG, and GitHub allows only ONE per OAuth app,
# so each environment needs its own app. Local: `http://127.0.0.1:54321/auth/v1/callback`.
# Hosted later: `https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback`.
#
# GitHub shows the client SECRET exactly once, at generation. There is no way to read it back — a
# lost secret is replaced, never recovered.
#
# `.env.local` ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH FOR THE SUPABASE CLI (measured 2026-08-09). `bunx supabase start`
# substitutes `env(...)` from the PROCESS environment and does not read this file, so a stack started
# without the values exported passes the LITERAL string `env(SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID)`
# into the authorize URL — no error, provider still advertised as enabled. Export them into the shell
# first, then start:
#   Get-Content .env.local | ForEach-Object { if ($_ -match '^\s*([A-Za-z0-9_]+)\s*=\s*(.+)$') { Set-Item "env:$($Matches[1])" $Matches[2].Trim() } }
#   bunx supabase stop ; bunx supabase start
SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GITHUB_SECRET=

SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
SUPABASE_URL=
VITE_SUPABASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=
